{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Document how to clone this repo cheaply, state the one accepted degradation honestly, and fix the\nlatent wrong-answer bug that degradation would otherwise cause. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Doc\nRecord `--filter=blob:none` guidance with the measured numbers (full **1.04 GiB / 59 s** vs blobless\n**68.7 MiB / 14.3 s**), and that `--filter=tree:0` must never be used here (measured **3.57 GiB**,\n3.4x larger than full). Note that rebar itself cannot filter a clone it does not perform.\n\n## The code fix (why it belongs with the doc)\n`git blame` over historical trees runs on a real rebar path: `metrics/blame.py:69` <-\n`_blame_file_commits` <- `derive_caused_by` (`:94`) <- `transition_close.py:514`, i.e. **on every bug\nclose without an explicit `--caused-by`**. Under a blobless promisor this faults blobs one at a time.\n\n**Prerequisite the earlier draft missed (plan-review G6 remediation): failure and emptiness are\ncurrently indistinguishable.** `_blame_file_commits` (`blame.py:67-79`) returns `[]` BOTH when the\ngit call fails (`if out is None: return []`) AND when a successful blame yields no SHAs. So\n\"abort on any per-path failure\" is **not implementable as stated** — there is nothing to detect.\nThis ticket must therefore FIRST change `_blame_file_commits` to return `None` on git failure and\n`[]` on a successful-but-empty result, and only then have `derive_caused_by` abort on `None`.\nTreating a legitimately empty file as a failure would suppress correct derivations.\n\nTotal failure is benign (`blame.py:37` returns `None`). **Partial failure is not:**\n`derive_caused_by` loops per path and tallies across all of them, so if blame succeeds on some paths\nand fails on others — entirely plausible with a partly-warm object store — the strict-majority test\nruns over a **subset** and can elect a culprit that would not have won over the full set. That writes\na **wrong `caused_by` link**, which is worse than writing none.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `_blame_file_commits` returns **`None`** on git failure and **`[]`** on a successful-but-empty\n      blame — the two are distinguishable. Tier: **unit** with both fixtures.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/metrics/test_blame.py -k distinguishes -v`.\n- [ ] `derive_caused_by` returns `None` when `_blame_file_commits` returns `None` for **any** path,\n      rather than tallying a partial set. Tier: **unit** injecting a per-path git failure.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/metrics/test_blame.py -k partial_failure -v`.\n- [ ] A path whose blame legitimately yields **zero** lines does **not** abort the derivation (the\n      negative control that keeps correct derivations working). Tier: **unit**.\n- [ ] No `caused_by` link event is written in that case. Tier: **integration** on the close path\n      asserting the event set.\n- [ ] With all paths succeeding, the derived culprit is unchanged from today — the negative control.\n      Tier: **unit** golden comparison.\n- [ ] The guidance doc exists, states the measured numbers, names the accepted `git blame`\n      degradation, and is linked from the docs index. Tier: **doc-existence + content check**.\n\n## Files\n`src/rebar/metrics/blame.py`, `docs/` guidance page, tests\n\nScope: S. No dependencies."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785376973844553001,"uuid":"aaa18242-a2ba-4cfa-b984-410dfb29439c"}